<p>HardPoint keeps stock per branch. Every sale, delivery, transfer and correction is a line in the stock history, so you can always see why
  a number is what it is. <strong>Stock › Stock levels</strong> shows what each branch holds and what it's worth, with stock on
  its way between branches on its own line.</p>

<h2>Correcting stock</h2>
<p>Open the product and press <strong>Adjust stock</strong>. Choose the branch, how many to add or take off, and the reason: opening stock,
  found, damaged, stolen, expired, internal use or a correction. Adjustments are in the activity log with who made them.</p>
<p>The till only sells what is on the shelf: once a product is down to zero at the branch, it can't be sold until the delivery is
  recorded or the stock adjusted, and the till says how many are left when a cashier tries to sell more. A kit needs all its parts.</p>
<p>A level can still go below zero when the offline till sold something that another till had already sold, since the goods have gone.
  <strong>Stock › Stock levels</strong> counts these for the branch; press <strong>Below zero</strong> to list them (the same
  <strong>Below zero</strong> filter is on the products list), then record the delivery or adjust the stock.</p>
<p>A product's unit, whether it tracks stock and whether it is a kit can't change while any branch has stock of it, since the quantities on
  hand would mean something else: adjust the stock to zero first.</p>

<h2>Moving stock between branches</h2>
<ol>
  <li>In <strong>Stock › Transfers</strong>, press <strong>New</strong>, choose the branches and the products, and press
    <strong>Send transfer</strong>. The stock leaves the sending branch and shows as in transit.</li>
  <li>When it arrives, the other branch counts it in. If anything is short, type what arrived against that line and say whether the
    rest was lost on the way (it is written off at the receiving branch) or never left (it goes back into the sending branch's
    stock), then press <strong>Mark received at …</strong>. What arrived is theirs, and the transfer shows what was short.</li>
  <li>While it is still in transit, <strong>Cancel transfer</strong> puts the stock back at the sending branch.</li>
</ol>

<%= render "help/walkthrough", tour: HelpTour.find("transfer") %>

<h2>Stock takes</h2>
<ol>
  <li>In <strong>Stock › Stock takes</strong>, press <strong>New</strong>. Count <strong>everything</strong>, or pick one category for a
    quicker cycle count, and press <strong>Start counting</strong>.</li>
  <li>Count the shelves: type quantities on the computer, scan with a barcode scanner, or
    <%= link_to "count on your phone", help_article_path("stock-on-your-phone"), class: "link" %>. Several people can count at once.</li>
  <li>Press <strong>Submit for approval</strong>. The <strong>Differences</strong> tab shows what's over or short and what it's worth at cost;
    <strong>Not counted</strong> shows what was missed.</li>
  <li>An owner or manager presses <strong>Approve and correct stock</strong>. Anything not counted is left as it was.</li>
</ol>
<p>Selling carries on during a stock take; HardPoint allows for what was sold while you counted.</p>

<%= render "help/walkthrough", tour: HelpTour.find("stock-take") %>

<h2>Knowing what to reorder</h2>
<p>Give products a <strong>reorder level</strong>. <strong>Stock › Reorder</strong> shows everything at or below it at a branch,
  and owners, managers and stock clerks get a low-stock email each morning. It also lists anything at zero that has no reorder level,
  since the till can't sell it and it would otherwise go unnoticed. To turn the list into orders, see
  <%= link_to "Buying from suppliers", help_article_path("buying-from-suppliers"), class: "link" %>.</p>
<p>A branch that sells a product faster or slower than the others can have its own level: on the product's page, type it under
  <strong>Reorder at</strong> next to the branch and press <strong>Set</strong>. Leave it blank to use the product's level.
  Stock already on order, or on its way from another branch, counts as coming, so it isn't suggested twice.</p>

<h2>Stock history</h2>
<p><strong>All movements</strong> on <strong>Stock › Stock levels</strong> lists every change to stock, filtered by product, branch and reason. Each product's page shows its
  latest movements too.</p>
